--expect
0,0,1,1,1,
--expect-why
DERIVED from the one rule in `eqalloc.kin`, applied to the driver's category
table. Nothing here was read off a run.

  eq-may-alloc rt v = (is-heap v) AND (category rt v != CAT_SCALAR)

The fixture makes both halves observable and INDEPENDENTLY so: `is-heap` is
`v >= 100`, and the heap values 100..103 carry categories 0,1,2,3 with
CAT_SCALAR = 0.

  5    not heap            -> the AND short-circuits            -> 0
  100  heap, category 0    -> 0 == CAT_SCALAR, so no allocation -> 0
  101  heap, category 1    -> != CAT_SCALAR                     -> 1
  102  heap, category 2    -> != CAT_SCALAR                     -> 1
  103  heap, category 3    -> != CAT_SCALAR                     -> 1

THE FIRST TWO ZEROS ARE THE CHECK, and they are zero for DIFFERENT reasons.
`5` is refused by `is-heap`; `100` passes `is-heap` and is refused by the
category test. A source that dropped the `is-heap` conjunct would still print
0 for 100 but would index `cats[5 - 100]` for the immediate and crash; one
that dropped the `!=` test would print 1 for 100. Either way the answer moves,
which is why the fixture has a heap value whose category IS scalar.

The direction matters and is not symmetric: this predicate gates ROOTING. A
spurious 1 costs six percent on `get` (the header's measurement); a spurious 0
drops rooting on a walk that really does allocate, and a collection in the
middle of a map lookup moves the objects the lookup is holding. So `0,0,1,1,1,`
is the safe answer AND the correct one, and a check that could not tell it from
`1,1,1,1,1,` would be no check.

The trailing comma is real: each driver appends `,` after every value and none
of them strips the last one, so the printed line genuinely ends in a comma.
